lesson: 108
title: Sides and corners of many shapes
stars: 1
explain: A triangle has 3 sides and 3 corners, a square 4, a pentagon 5 and a hexagon 6: a flat shape has as many corners as sides.
explain: To count the sides of many shapes, multiply each kind by its number of sides and add: 2 triangles and 3 squares have 2 × 3 + 3 × 4 = 18 sides.
vars: a=name x=2..9 y=2..9 z=2..9 h=2..9
answer: 3 * x + 4 * y + 5 * z + 6 * h
exercise: {a} draws {x} triangles, {y} squares, {z} pentagons and {h} hexagons. How many sides do the shapes have in all?
exercise: There are {x} triangles, {y} squares, {z} pentagons and {h} hexagons on a page. How many corners are there altogether?
exercise: {a} makes {x} triangles, {y} squares, {z} pentagons and {h} hexagons from sticks, one stick for each side. How many sticks does {a.he} use?
exercise: How many sides do {x} triangles, {y} squares, {z} pentagons and {h} hexagons have together?
exercise: {a} puts a dot on every corner of {x} triangles, {y} squares, {z} pentagons and {h} hexagons. How many dots does {a.he} draw?
test: On a poster there are {h} hexagons, {z} pentagons, {y} squares and {x} triangles. Count all the corners: how many are there?
test: {a} cuts out {y} squares and {h} hexagons, then {x} triangles and {z} pentagons. Every side gets a strip of tape. How many strips are needed?
test: A floor pattern uses {z} pentagons, {x} triangles, {h} hexagons and {y} squares. What is the total number of sides?
test: {a} has {x} triangle cards, {y} square cards, {z} pentagon cards and {h} hexagon cards. A star goes on each corner of every card. How many stars are used?
exercise: How many corners do {x} triangles, {y} squares, {z} pentagons and {h} hexagons have altogether?
exercise: {a} in Utah cuts out {h} hexagons, {z} pentagons, {y} squares and {x} triangles. How many sides are there in total?
exercise: Count the sides of {x} triangles, {y} squares, {z} pentagons and {h} hexagons. How many are there?
exercise: A quilt in Yorkshire has {x} triangle patches, {y} square patches, {z} pentagon patches and {h} hexagon patches. How many corners do the patches have combined?
exercise: {a} builds {x.words} triangles, {y.words} squares, {z.words} pentagons and {h.words} hexagons with matchsticks, one matchstick per side. How many matchsticks does {a.he} need?
exercise: What is the total number of sides of {y} squares, {x} triangles, {h} hexagons and {z} pentagons?
exercise: {a} in Canberra puts a bead on every corner of {x} triangles, {y} squares, {z} pentagons and {h} hexagons. How many beads does {a.he} use?
exercise: On a page there are {z} pentagons, {h} hexagons, {x} triangles and {y} squares. How many corners are on the page in all?
test: {a} traces every side of {h} hexagons, {x} triangles, {z} pentagons and {y} squares in red. How many red lines does {a.he} draw?
test: A mosaic in Toronto uses {y} square tiles, {z} pentagon tiles, {x} triangle tiles and {h} hexagon tiles. Altogether, how many corners do the tiles have?
test: {a} from Galway sticks a gold star on each corner of {x} triangles, {h} hexagons, {y} squares and {z} pentagons. How many gold stars does {a.he} use?
test: Add up the sides of these shapes: {z} pentagons, {y} squares, {h} hexagons and {x} triangles. What is the sum?
test: A kite maker in Wellington needs one stick for every side of {x} triangles, {y} squares, {z} pentagons and {h} hexagons. How many sticks is that?
test: How many corners must {a} count on {h} hexagons, {y} squares, {x} triangles and {z} pentagons?
